### Notes:

Yes, Arduino throws many specific compilation errors before displaying the generic Error during build: exit status 1 message. 
The phrase exit status 1 is simply a summary stating that the underlying C++ compiler (GCC) failed.
The real, descriptive errors are printed in the console panel right above that final status message.
The actual compilation errors you will see depend on what is wrong with the code: Standard C++ Syntax ErrorsBecause Arduino code is essentially C++, the Arduino IDE displays classic compiler warnings and errors:expected ';' before '}' token: You forgot a semicolon at the end of a line.
'variable_name' was not declared in this scope: You misspelled a variable or forgot to define it before use.expected '}' at end of input: You have an unmatched opening curly brace { somewhere in your code.redefinition of 'void setup()': You accidentally pasted the setup() or loop() functions twice.
File and Library Errorsfatal error: LibraryName.h: No such file or directory: You are attempting to include a library that is not installed in your Arduino IDE Library Manager.
Linker ErrorsSometimes code compiles fine but fails during assembly (linking):undefined reference to 'setup' or 'loop': Your code is missing one of the two core required Arduino functions.collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status: The compiler completed its job, but the linker failed to combine the files.Memory Overflow ErrorsNot enough memory; see...: Your sketch uses more RAM (dynamic memory) than your physical Arduino board possesses.Sketch too big; see...: Your program's compiled flash memory size exceeds the storage capacity of the selected board.How to See the Real ErrorsIf your IDE only shows exit status 1, you can unhide the detailed error messages using the Arduino Help Center Guide:Open File > Preferences.Look for Show verbose output during.Check the box for compilation.Re-verify your code to see the full, actionable error log in the bottom console.
